WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.096 --> 00:00:17.421
Hello, and welcome to Superstars in History.

2
00:00:17.962 --> 00:00:19.662
I'm your host, Faye Moss.

3
00:00:20.703 --> 00:00:26.285
Today's guest knows better than most people that it's not easy being a queen!

4
00:00:27.305 --> 00:00:34.961
Her reign is viewed now as a golden age, but her path to the crown was surprising—and sometimes

5
00:00:35.241 --> 00:00:36.481
even dangerous.

6
00:00:37.422 --> 00:00:44.586
Please give a royal welcome to Her Majesty Queen Elizabeth I of England and Ireland!

7
00:01:00.454 --> 00:01:03.156
Thank you, child, for inviting me to your court.

8
00:01:04.104 --> 00:01:06.005
Of course, Your Majesty.

9
00:01:07.306 --> 00:01:09.847
You were born in 1533.

10
00:01:10.608 --> 00:01:14.790
Through most of your childhood, it seemed unlikely that you would become queen.

11
00:01:16.111 --> 00:01:17.411
And then you did!

12
00:01:17.431 --> 00:01:21.113
Please tell us how you got there.

13
00:01:23.054 --> 00:01:27.777
I was the first child of King Henry VIII of England and Anne Boleyn.

14
00:01:29.218 --> 00:01:34.317
As their daughter, I was a princess and heir to the throne, but no one thought I would

15
00:01:34.397 --> 00:01:38.179
ever become queen—England had never crowned a queen before!

16
00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:45.763
In fact, my father had divorced his first wife because she hadn't given birth to a son.

17
00:01:46.643 --> 00:01:50.405
Their only child was a daughter, my half sister, Mary.

18
00:01:51.406 --> 00:01:55.068
It sounds like your father really wanted a son!

19
00:01:56.108 --> 00:02:01.811
Yes, my father was desperate to have a male heir who would succeed him one day.

20
00:02:02.672 --> 00:02:06.050
Alas, he was married to my mother for only a few years.

21
00:02:07.091 --> 00:02:13.376
I was just two when she became the victim of a political plot and was jailed and beheaded.

22
00:02:14.757 --> 00:02:16.018
That's awful!

23
00:02:17.599 --> 00:02:19.020
Indeed it was, Faye.

24
00:02:20.041 --> 00:02:23.924
However, my father loved me and treated me well.

25
00:02:25.125 --> 00:02:29.228
I was given an excellent education and learned several languages.

26
00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:34.681
I received the kind of education a male heir to the throne would have had.

27
00:02:35.962 --> 00:02:42.263
A male heir was finally born to my father and his third wife, Jane Seymour, who died shortly

28
00:02:42.403 --> 00:02:43.924
after Prince Edward was born.

29
00:02:44.844 --> 00:02:50.506
My father married three more times in the hope of having more sons, but it was not meant

30
00:02:50.546 --> 00:02:50.886
to be.

31
00:02:51.826 --> 00:02:55.467
When he died in 1547, nine-year-old Edward became King Edward VI.

32
00:02:59.748 --> 00:03:04.591
After less than seven years on the throne, Edward fell ill and died.

33
00:03:05.692 --> 00:03:07.673
Mary then became queen.

34
00:03:08.814 --> 00:03:11.636
That made you next in line to the throne.

35
00:03:12.617 --> 00:03:14.638
What were those years like for you?

36
00:03:16.159 --> 00:03:17.540
They were dangerous!

37
00:03:18.941 --> 00:03:23.844
To divorce his first wife, my father had separated England from the Roman Catholic Church.

38
00:03:24.845 --> 00:03:29.328
This created a lot of religious tension at court and in the country.

39
00:03:31.640 --> 00:03:37.165
Mary had remained a Catholic and returned England to the Catholic Church when she became queen.

40
00:03:38.146 --> 00:03:43.771
In addition she married Prince Philip of Spain, also a Catholic, and hoped to have a son.

41
00:03:44.852 --> 00:03:51.098
Since I had a claim to the throne but wasn't thought to be a Catholic, Mary was suspicious

42
00:03:51.158 --> 00:03:51.498
of me.

43
00:03:52.539 --> 00:03:57.344
After a rebellion, in which I played no part, I was kept under arrest.

44
00:03:58.364 --> 00:04:02.627
Eventually, when she thought she was pregnant and would have an heir, I was released.

45
00:04:03.608 --> 00:04:05.789
However, her child never came.

46
00:04:07.170 --> 00:04:10.012
Those must have been difficult years for you.

47
00:04:11.033 --> 00:04:17.397
I privately opposed my sister's religious policy but had to be careful to always show my loyalty to

48
00:04:17.477 --> 00:04:23.221
her. I developed a lot of diplomatic skill during these years that served me well later.

49
00:04:24.582 --> 00:04:27.564
I learned that it is often best to keep silent.

50
00:04:28.520 --> 00:04:33.724
And when one does speak, it's prudent not to say everything that one is thinking.

51
00:04:34.725 --> 00:04:40.569
In the fourth year of her reign, Mary thought she was pregnant again, but no child came, and

52
00:04:40.629 --> 00:04:42.911
it was clear that she was in poor health.

53
00:04:44.031 --> 00:04:48.855
In 1558 she died, and I became Queen of England and Ireland.

54
00:04:49.896 --> 00:04:51.497
I was 25 years old.

55
00:04:52.718 --> 00:04:55.520
Unlike Queen Mary, you never married.

56
00:04:56.636 --> 00:05:01.938
Marriage and having children was important, but it came with risks, especially for a woman.

57
00:05:02.898 --> 00:05:07.119
I would have had to share my power with my husband, to whom I was expected to be

58
00:05:07.319 --> 00:05:13.141
obedient. My sister's marriage to a foreign royal had been unpopular.

59
00:05:14.041 --> 00:05:18.422
But choosing one Englishman could have created resentment among them all.

60
00:05:19.402 --> 00:05:23.283
Rather than marry a man, I was married to my country.

61
00:05:24.204 --> 00:05:28.214
As you can see, I wear my coronation ring on my wedding finger.

62
00:05:30.615 --> 00:05:34.597
I guess your decision helped keep England stable during your reign.

63
00:05:35.738 --> 00:05:40.360
Ensuring stability was the most important task I faced as queen.

64
00:05:41.361 --> 00:05:46.144
For many years England had suffered a series of power struggles and civil wars.

65
00:05:47.364 --> 00:05:53.388
I was able to bring a moderation to the country that kept factions from tearing us in two.

66
00:05:54.376 --> 00:06:01.102
I firmly established the Church of England, but unlike Mary, I made changes gradually, and with the love

67
00:06:01.162 --> 00:06:02.543
and support of the people.

68
00:06:03.564 --> 00:06:08.728
Not only did I seek to prevent wars at home, but it was also my aim to avoid

69
00:06:08.828 --> 00:06:09.689
foreign wars.

70
00:06:10.830 --> 00:06:15.033
Yes, but you had a close call with Spain.

71
00:06:15.254 --> 00:06:23.833
In 1588 Spain sent a great fleet of warships, the Spanish Armada, to overthrow me and return England

72
00:06:23.893 --> 00:06:24.954
to the Catholic Church.

73
00:06:26.094 --> 00:06:32.839
Some feared that because I was a woman—a queen and not a king—I was too weak to lead

74
00:06:32.879 --> 00:06:34.360
the country through this crisis.

75
00:06:35.541 --> 00:06:37.162
I proved them wrong.

76
00:06:38.323 --> 00:06:43.386
I spoke to the troops, encouraged them to protect our shores, and assured them that they would be

77
00:06:43.446 --> 00:06:44.827
rewarded for doing so.

78
00:06:45.888 --> 00:06:51.952
The English navy defended our shores, then the heavens sent a storm to finish off the Armada.

79
00:06:53.148 --> 00:06:57.669
That victory is remembered as one of the greatest accomplishments of your reign.

80
00:06:58.570 --> 00:07:03.171
Your reign also saw the beginnings of what would become a huge empire.

81
00:07:04.171 --> 00:07:10.113
Under my rule Sir Francis Drake became the second man to circumnavigate the globe.

82
00:07:11.193 --> 00:07:16.755
And Sir Walter Raleigh went to the New World and claimed a huge part of it for England.

83
00:07:17.895 --> 00:07:24.335
Spain had been the most powerful country in the world until the brave sailors of my realm challenged

84
00:07:24.435 --> 00:07:27.457
Spanish dominance of the seas and the New World.

85
00:07:28.638 --> 00:07:31.500
England established itself as a naval power.

86
00:07:32.721 --> 00:07:36.264
England also saw a flowering of arts and culture.

87
00:07:37.325 --> 00:07:39.667
You were even a writer yourself.

88
00:07:41.028 --> 00:07:46.232
I translated many works throughout my life, and from time to time, I drafted poems.

89
00:07:47.388 --> 00:07:51.910
But my more important role was to inspire other writers and artists.

90
00:07:53.090 --> 00:08:00.694
We were blessed with gifted composers, painters, playwrights, and poets— including the talented Mr.

91
00:08:00.794 --> 00:08:03.034
Shakespeare, whose plays I adored.

92
00:08:03.915 --> 00:08:09.297
My tender care of my people gave them the conditions necessary to create art.

93
00:08:10.297 --> 00:08:13.159
Thank you so much for being with us today, Your Majesty.

94
00:08:14.199 --> 00:08:16.280
I'll let you get back to history now.

95
00:08:17.568 --> 00:08:18.509
Thank you, Faye.

96
00:08:21.611 --> 00:08:29.498
Queen Elizabeth reigned for over 44 years—one of the longest reigns of an English monarch at the time.

97
00:08:30.599 --> 00:08:34.342
She died in 1603 at the age of 69.

98
00:08:35.463 --> 00:08:43.269
Her relative King James VI of Scotland succeeded her, becoming James I of England and uniting the two

99
00:08:43.369 --> 00:08:53.554
kingdoms. The period of her reign, known as the Elizabethan Age, is still remembered for English expansion and

100
00:08:53.714 --> 00:08:56.335
as a golden age of English culture.

101
00:08:57.295 --> 00:08:58.456
That's all for today.

102
00:08:58.896 --> 00:09:03.717
Join me next week when I'll speak with another of history's superstars.
